1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,096
It is Sunday evening, and Aziz sits at his laptop.

2
00:00:03,146 --> 00:00:07,560
He opens the marketplace web profile to see where his new kettle is.

3
00:00:07,610 --> 00:00:11,928
He types his phone number, then his password, and the password is right.

4
00:00:11,978 --> 00:00:13,968
But the page does not open his order.

5
00:00:14,018 --> 00:00:16,992
It asks him to approve the login in the mobile bank.

6
00:00:17,042 --> 00:00:21,872
The password is right, so why is it not enough?

7
00:00:21,922 --> 00:00:29,768
In Lesson 2, Aziz became one person, with one phone number, one password, and one session on each device.

8
00:00:29,818 --> 00:00:37,112
This lesson gives the three words that identity work uses for this moment, and it explains what a level really is.

9
00:00:37,162 --> 00:00:39,536
Each word answers a different question.

10
00:00:39,586 --> 00:00:41,792
The first word is authentication.

11
00:00:41,842 --> 00:00:45,416
Authentication asks: is this the owner of the account?

12
00:00:45,466 --> 00:00:50,840
Aziz answers with something he knows, the password, and something he has, his phone.

13
00:00:50,890 --> 00:00:56,120
On his phone, the mobile bank shows the request, with Approve and This was not me.

14
00:00:56,170 --> 00:00:59,768
In other cases, the phone gets a one-time code instead.

15
00:00:59,818 --> 00:01:02,408
Notice what authentication does not say.

16
00:01:02,458 --> 00:01:05,600
It does not say that this is Aziz from the state records.

17
00:01:05,650 --> 00:01:09,056
It says only that this is the person who registered the account.

18
00:01:09,106 --> 00:01:15,368
A stranger can register with their own phone and their own password, and pass authentication perfectly every day.

19
00:01:15,418 --> 00:01:17,696
The second word is identity proofing.

20
00:01:17,746 --> 00:01:22,952
Identity proofing asks: is this the real person in the state records?

21
00:01:23,002 --> 00:01:27,680
heeyID does it in one way only: MyID, in the master app.

22
00:01:27,730 --> 00:01:31,928
MyID compares a live face and a document with the state records.

23
00:01:31,978 --> 00:01:35,312
Aziz did it one time, in the mobile bank, last week.

24
00:01:35,362 --> 00:01:37,424
Proofing is slow and rare.

25
00:01:37,474 --> 00:01:41,936
Authentication is fast, and it happens at every login.

26
00:01:41,986 --> 00:01:48,176
I think the most common mistake in identity work is to treat a strong login as proof of a person.

27
00:01:48,226 --> 00:01:50,048
It is not.

28
00:01:50,098 --> 00:01:54,320
A password and a code prove that you hold the account, not who you are.

29
00:01:54,370 --> 00:01:56,480
The third word is authorisation.

30
00:01:56,530 --> 00:02:00,056
Authorisation asks: what may this session do, now?

31
00:02:00,106 --> 00:02:06,632
The marketplace must see level two before it shows personal data, like orders, addresses or cards.

32
00:02:06,682 --> 00:02:08,912
That is the rule that stopped the page.

33
00:02:08,962 --> 00:02:10,784
So what is a level?

34
00:02:10,834 --> 00:02:12,656
heeyID has three.

35
00:02:12,706 --> 00:02:15,464
Level zero: nothing is verified.

36
00:02:15,514 --> 00:02:19,184
Level one: a one-time code verified the phone number.

37
00:02:19,234 --> 00:02:24,872
Level two: MyID proved the person against the state records, and the phone number is confirmed.

38
00:02:24,922 --> 00:02:27,824
Only the check for a level gives that level.

39
00:02:27,874 --> 00:02:32,648
No operator and no setting can put a person at level two without MyID.

40
00:02:32,698 --> 00:02:35,648
Now the part that surprises most developers.

41
00:02:35,698 --> 00:02:39,176
A person has a level, and a session has a level too.

42
00:02:39,226 --> 00:02:42,176
Aziz, the person, is at level two.

43
00:02:42,226 --> 00:02:47,000
But a session gets level two only on a trusted device of a person at level two.

44
00:02:47,050 --> 00:02:50,768
A trusted device is a phone where the person passed MyID.

45
00:02:50,818 --> 00:02:52,640
His phone is one.

46
00:02:52,690 --> 00:02:54,512
A browser is never one.

47
00:02:54,562 --> 00:03:01,568
So the laptop session can reach level two in one way only: Aziz approves it in the master app, on his trusted phone.

48
00:03:01,618 --> 00:03:03,440
Back to Sunday evening.

49
00:03:03,490 --> 00:03:07,736
The request on his phone shows the browser, the network address and the time.

50
00:03:07,786 --> 00:03:09,608
Aziz taps Approve.

51
00:03:09,658 --> 00:03:13,448
The laptop session gets level two, and the order page opens.

52
00:03:13,498 --> 00:03:15,320
Now take a different evening.

53
00:03:15,370 --> 00:03:19,160
Aziz has no session in the mobile bank, so nobody can approve.

54
00:03:19,210 --> 00:03:24,560
After the password, the bank's SMS service sends him a code, and he types it.

55
00:03:24,610 --> 00:03:27,056
He is in: authentication passed.

56
00:03:27,106 --> 00:03:31,256
But the laptop session is at level one, and the order stays closed.

57
00:03:31,306 --> 00:03:33,656
The marketplace does not refuse him.

58
00:03:33,706 --> 00:03:38,144
It asks for a step-up: it names the level it needs, and the check that gives it.

59
00:03:38,194 --> 00:03:41,672
Same person, same password, two different answers.

60
00:03:41,722 --> 00:03:44,744
The difference is the session, not the person.

61
00:03:44,794 --> 00:03:48,392
Two more names, and you can read any identity document.

62
00:03:48,442 --> 00:03:55,376
OAuth 2.1 is the open standard for authorisation: how an app gets a token, and what the token lets it do.

63
00:03:55,426 --> 00:04:01,208
OpenID Connect sits on top of it, and adds authentication: who logged in, and how.

64
00:04:01,258 --> 00:04:04,112
heeyID has no token format of its own.

65
00:04:04,162 --> 00:04:09,656
Keycloak issues standard tokens, and each token carries the level of its session.

66
00:04:09,706 --> 00:04:13,832
So the marketplace reads the level from the token, and decides.

67
00:04:13,882 --> 00:04:17,552
To recap: authentication asks if this is the owner.

68
00:04:17,602 --> 00:04:21,416
Identity proofing asks if this is the real person.

69
00:04:21,466 --> 00:04:24,032
Authorisation asks what the session may do.

70
00:04:24,082 --> 00:04:27,440
A person has a level, and each session has its own.

71
00:04:27,490 --> 00:04:31,700
So why did the right password not open the order?

72
00:04:31,750 --> 00:04:34,628
Because a password answers only the first question.

73
00:04:34,678 --> 00:04:37,268
These checks touch passports and phone numbers.

74
00:04:37,318 --> 00:04:40,172
So where does heeyID run, and whom may it call?

75
00:04:40,222 --> 00:04:41,924
Next: the perimeter.
